# 11. Interview Q&A Preparation

### Q: "Design a Rate Limiter" (THE classic question)

> *"I've actually built one. Let me walk you through it. I implemented 4 algorithms — Token Bucket for burst-friendly APIs, Sliding Window Log for strict accuracy, Fixed Window Counter for simplicity, and Sliding Window Counter for the best accuracy-to-memory ratio. Each runs as a Redis Lua script for atomic distributed counting. The algorithm is configurable per tenant and per endpoint via the Strategy Pattern. I load-tested it with Gatling — 10K+ requests per second with sub-millisecond rate limit check latency."*

### Q: "Why Redis Lua scripts?"

> *"Without Lua, rate limiting requires GET + compare + INCR — three separate Redis commands. Between GET and INCR, another thread could modify the counter, causing a race condition. Lua scripts execute atomically inside Redis — the entire check-and-increment happens as one operation. Even with 100 gateway instances hitting Redis simultaneously, there are zero race conditions."*

### Q: "Which algorithm is best?"

> *"It depends. Token Bucket for APIs where bursts are acceptable — like a chat API. Sliding Window Counter for general-purpose rate limiting — best accuracy-to-memory ratio. Sliding Window Log when you need perfect accuracy and can afford the memory — like a payment API. Fixed Window Counter when simplicity matters and edge bursts are acceptable."*

### Q: "How does it work across multiple gateway instances?"

> *"All instances share the same Redis. Rate limit counters are stored in Redis, not in gateway memory. When any instance checks a rate limit, it executes a Lua script against the shared Redis — so the counter is always globally accurate. This is what makes it truly distributed."*

